WEBVTT

NOTE Module 02 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (116.6s). en.

1
00:00:00.908 --> 00:00:03.385
Welcome to Module Two — neural network fundamentals.

2
00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:13.255
We'll build the network from the ground up: the artificial neuron, activation functions, the forward and backward pass, and the shape of a Keras training loop.

3
00:00:14.582 --> 00:00:17.601
It starts with the perceptron — a single neuron.

4
00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:22.936
It weights its inputs, sums them, adds a bias, and passes the result through an activation.

5
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:29.814
On its own it can only draw a straight line, so it can't even solve XOR.

6
00:00:30.153 --> 00:00:34.912
But stack neurons into hidden layers, and a multi-layer perceptron becomes a universal function approximator.

7
00:00:35.252 --> 00:00:39.075
The activation is what gives the network its power.

8
00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:42.400
Sigmoid and tanh saturate and choke the gradient.

9
00:00:42.747 --> 00:00:50.155
ReLU is the cheap, sparse workhorse you'll reach for by default, and GELU is a smoother cousin popular in Transformers.

10
00:00:50.504 --> 00:00:54.550
Pick the activation and the initializer together — He for ReLU.

11
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.198
So how does it learn?

12
00:00:57.781 --> 00:01:02.367
The forward pass computes a loss from the inputs and the current weights.

13
00:01:02.698 --> 00:01:10.748
The backward pass walks the chain rule in reverse, where each layer contributes a local Jacobian — really just a matrix multiply.

14
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:17.685
The optimizer then nudges every weight, and GradientTape makes that whole tape visible when you need to debug it.

15
00:01:18.016 --> 00:01:23.057
In Keras you'll meet two front doors to the same engine.

16
00:01:23.395 --> 00:01:27.620
The Sequential API stacks layers in a line — fastest to prototype.

17
00:01:27.949 --> 00:01:31.242
The Functional API builds arbitrary graphs with multiple inputs and outputs.

18
00:01:31.572 --> 00:01:34.909
And subclassing the Model gives you full research freedom.

19
00:01:36.566 --> 00:01:52.312
One last thing makes deep networks actually trainable: normalize your inputs to mean zero and unit variance, let batch norm steady the activations in between, and match your initializer to your activation — because bad initialization is exactly how gradients vanish or explode.

20
00:01:52.656 --> 00:01:56.479
Let's build your first MLP — open the labs.
